"**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines that covers all required points and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence such a configuration is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. List the six points that must be covered:  \n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\]  \n2. Observe that a *sunny* line cannot be horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), or have slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n3. Show that any sunny line can contain **at most two** of the six points; otherwise three collinear points would have one of the forbidden slopes.  \n4. Consequently, the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points, so the remaining (non\u2011sunny) line must cover at least two points.  \n5. Enumerate all non\u2011sunny lines that contain at least two of the six points (they are \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\)).  \n6. For each of these six possibilities, list the points left uncovered and verify that **no** pair of the remaining points can lie on a sunny line (every pair has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover the leftovers.  \n7. Since every admissible choice for the non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nAny sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nBecause the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points in \\(S\\) are \\(1,2,3\\) and the \\(y\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,3\\), the only possible slopes for a line through two points of \\(S\\) are\n\n\\[\n0,\\;\\infty,\\;-1,\\;1,\\;\\frac12,\\;2,\\;-\\frac12,\\;-2 .\n\\]\n\nIf three points of \\(S\\) are collinear, the slope must be the same for each pair.  \nThe only slopes that occur for **three** points of \\(S\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=c\\)).  \nAll other slopes appear for at most two points.  \nSince a sunny line is prohibited from having slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), it cannot contain three points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Consequence of Lemma 1  \nWith two sunny lines we can cover at most \\(2\\cdot 2 =4\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nHence the remaining (non\u2011sunny) line must cover at least \\(6-4 =2\\) points.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nThe only non\u2011sunny lines that contain at least two points of \\(S\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=1,\\;y=2,\\\\\n&x+y=3,\\;x+y=4 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), or has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nChecking each possible constant \\(c\\) that yields points of \\(S\\):\n\n* Vertical: \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); \\(x=2\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\); \\(x=3\\) contains only \\((3,1)\\).\n* Horizontal: \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); \\(y=2\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\); \\(y=3\\) contains only \\((1,3)\\).\n* Slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=2\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\); \\(x+y=3\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\); \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nThus the six listed lines are exactly those non\u2011sunny lines that contain at least two points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nWe now examine each of the six possibilities for the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{0}\\) and show that the remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1: \\(L_{0}=x=1\\)  \n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll unordered pairs among the remaining points have slopes:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(2,1)-(2,2):\\ \\infty\\ (\\text{vertical}),\\\\\n&(2,1)-(3,1):\\ 0\\ (\\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n&(2,2)-(3,1):\\ -1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach slope is forbidden for a sunny line. Hence no sunny line can contain any pair of the remaining points; each sunny line could cover at most one of them, leaving at least one uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(L_{0}=x=2\\)  \n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).\n\nPairs and their slopes:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,2):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,1)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,2)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,1)-(3,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n&(1,2)-(3,1):\\ -\\frac12\\ (\\text{allowed}),\\\\\n&(1,3)-(3,1):\\ -1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOnly the pair \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) has an allowed slope. Suppose one sunny line \\(L_{1}\\) is the line through these two points; its equation is \\(y=-\\frac12x+\\frac32\\).  \nThe two points left uncovered are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\). Any line through both is vertical (\\(x=1\\)), which is non\u2011sunny. Hence the second sunny line cannot cover both; it can cover at most one, leaving a point uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 3: \\(L_{0}=y=1\\)  \n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).\n\nSlopes of all pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,2)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,2)-(2,2):\\ 0,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,2):\\ -1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll are forbidden; thus no sunny line can contain two of the remaining points. With only two sunny lines we could cover at most two of them, leaving at least one uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 4: \\(L_{0}=y=2\\)  \n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).\n\nPairs and slopes:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,1)-(2,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n&(1,1)-(3,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,1):\\ -2,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(3,1):\\ -1,\\\\\n&(2,1)-(3,1):\\ 0 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOnly the pair \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) has an allowed slope \\(-2\\). Let \\(L_{1}\\) be the line through these two points; its equation is \\(y=-2x+5\\).  \nThe points still uncovered are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); any line through them is horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), which is non\u2011sunny. Hence the second sunny line cannot cover both. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 5: \\(L_{0}=x+y=3\\)  \n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nSlopes of all pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,1)-(2,2):\\ 1\\ (\\text{allowed}),\\\\\n&(1,1)-(3,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,2):\\ -1,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(3,1):\\ -1,\\\\\n&(2,2)-(3,1):\\ -1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOnly the pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) yields an allowed slope \\(1\\). Let \\(L_{1}\\) be the line \\(y=x\\) through them.  \nThe two points left are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); any line through both has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden. Hence the second sunny line cannot cover both. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 6: \\(L_{0}=x+y=4\\)  \n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\nSlopes of all pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,2):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n&(1,1)-(2,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n&(1,2)-(2,1):\\ -1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll are forbidden; thus no sunny line can contain two of the remaining points. With two sunny lines we could cover at most two of them, leaving one uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\nSince every possible choice for the non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, **no** arrangement of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
