er, so not covering (2,1) or (2,2). So L1 covers only those two points.\n\nL2: (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1, equation y-2 = 1(x-1) => y = x+1. So L2 passes through (1,2), (2,3), (3,4) out. So L2 covers (1,2) and (2,3). Also (3,4) out.\n\nL3: (1,3)-(4,1) slope -2/3, equation y-3 = -2/3 (x-1) => y = -2/3 x + 11/3. For x=2, y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3 not integer. x=3 => y = -2 + 11/3 = 5/3 not integer. So L3 covers only (1,3) and (4,1). L4: (1,4) plus maybe other points.\n\nNow uncovered points: (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Also (2,2) maybe can be covered by L4: line through (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2, same as before. That covers (2,2). Then (2,1) and (3,1) remain uncovered. Could we adjust L4 to cover (2,1) and (3,1)? Let's try line through (1,4) and (3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2, sunny. That covers (1,4) and (3,1). Does it also cover (2,1)? Check line equation: y-4 = -3/2 (x-1) => y = -3/2 x + 11/2. For x=2, y = -3 + 11/2 = 5/2 = 2.5, not integer. So (2,1) remains uncovered.\n\nThus with this pairing, we cannot cover all interior points with only 4 lines. Perhaps we need to assign interior points to lines differently: maybe we can assign (2,1) to line L2 (which currently is (1,2)-(2,3)). Does line L2 contain (2,1)? No, slope 1 line through (1,2) is y = x+1, so (2,1) not on it. Could we change L2 to be line through (1,2) and (2,1) instead? That would be slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, which is forbidden (slope -1). So cannot.\n\nMaybe we can assign (2,1) to line L3? L3 currently is (1,3)-(4,1). Does it contain (2,1)? Let's check slope -2/3 line: y = -2/3 x + 11/3. For x=2, y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3, not 1. So no.\n\nAlternatively, we could change the pairing of column 1 points to main diagonal points to allow interior points to be covered by the same lines. For instance, we could pair (1,1) with D2 (2,3) as before, (1,2) with D3 (3,2), (1,3) with D4 (4,1). Then lines are:\n\nL1: (1,1)-(2,3) slope 2, equation y = 2x -1. This line also passes through (3,5) out. So only (1,1) and (2,3). L2: (1,2)-(3,2) slope 0, horizontal? Wait (1,2)-(3,2) slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot pair (1,2) with D3 because that yields horizontal line. So we must avoid that pairing. Indeed, (1,2) with D3 (3,2) is forbidden because b = n+1-c? Let's check: b=2, c=3 => n+1-c = 5-3=2 => b = n+1-c, so it's forbidden. So we must avoid that pairing.\n\nThus the pairing must avoid forbidden pairs. The earlier pairing (1,1)-(2,3), (1,2)-(4,1), (1,3)-(3,2) avoids forbidden pairs. That seems the only possible pairing for n=4? Let's see all possible derangements for n=4: we need a bijection f: {1,2,3} -> {2,3,4} avoiding f(1)=4, f(2)=3, f(3)=2. The possible permutations of {2,3,4} are 6. Let's list them:\n\n1) (2,3,4) -> f(1)=2 (allowed), f(2)=3 (forbidden), f(3)=4 (allowed). So not allowed because f(2)=3 is forbidden.\n\n2) (2,4,3) -> f(1)=2 allowed, f(2)=4 allowed, f(3)=3 allowed (since f(3)=3 is not forbidden? Wait forbidden for b=3 is c=2, not 3. So allowed). So this is the earlier pairing: (1,1)-(2,3), (1,2)-(4,1), (1,3)-(3,2). Good.\n\n3) (3,2,4) -> f(1)=3 (forbidden? For b=1, forbidden c=4, so c=3 is allowed), f(2)=2 (forbidden? For b=2, forbidden c=3, so c=2 is allowed), f(3)=4 (forbidden? For b=3, forbidden c=2, so c=4 is allowed). So this is another allowed pairing: (1,1)-(3,2), (1,2)-(2,3), (1,3)-(4,1). Let's examine that.\n\n4) (3,4,2) -> f(1)=3 allowed, f(2)=4 allowed, f(3)=2 forbidden (b=3, c=2 is forbidden). So not allowed.\n\n5) (4,2,3) -> f(1)=4 forbidden (b=1, c=4 is forbidden). So not allowed.\n\n6) (4,3,2) -> f(1)=4 forbidden. So not allowed.\n\nThus there are two allowed pairings: (2,4,3) and (3,2,4). Let's examine the second allowed pairing (3,2,4): (1,1)-(3,2), (1,2)-(2,3), (1,3)-(4,1). Let's see lines:\n\n- L1: (1,1)-(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2, line y = (x+1)/2, covers (1,1) and (3,2). (2,?) not integer.\n\n- L2: (1,2)-(2,3) slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, line y = x+1, covers (1,2), (2,3). (3,4) out.\n\n- L3: (1,3)-(4,1) slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, line y = -2/3 x + 11/3, covers (1,3) and (4,1). (2,?) y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3 not integer; (3,?) y = -2 + 11/3 = 5/3 not integer.\n\nThus same coverage as before but different assignment. Still interior points (2,1),(2,2),(3,1) remain uncovered.\n\nThus any pairing will leave interior points uncovered, unless we can assign interior points to lines that also contain column 1 points and main diagonal points. But each line currently contains exactly two points: a column 1 point and a main diagonal point. We have one extra line (line for D1) that can be used to cover interior points. However, we have only one extra line, but there are multiple interior points. So we need to assign interior points to lines that already contain column 1 points and main diagonal points, i.e., we need lines that contain three points: a column 1 point, a main diagonal point, and an interior point. That would be possible if the three points are collinear. So we need to find a pairing such that the line through (1,b) and (c,n+1-c) also passes through some interior point (a,b').\n\nThus we need to find a pairing where the line through (1,b) and (c,n+1-c) passes through some other point (a,b') in the triangle. That would help cover interior points without extra lines. Let's see if possible.\n\nGeneral line through (1,b) and (c,n+1-c). Equation: slope m = (n+1-c - b)/(c-1). The line passes through (a,b') if b' - b = m (a - 1). So b' = b + m (a-1). Since m is rational, we need integer solutions.\n\nWe can try to find a pairing that yields integer points for interior points.\n\nLet's attempt for n=4: choose pairing (1,1)-(2,3) as before. Slope m = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2. So line is y = 2x -1. Does it pass through any interior point besides (2,3) and (1,1)? For a=3, y = 5, out. So no interior point.\n\nPair (1,2)-(4,1): slope m = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3. Equation y = -1/3 x + 7/3. For a=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 not integer. a=3 => y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3 not integer. So no interior point.\n\nPair (1,3)-(3,2): slope m = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2. Equation y = -1/2 x + 7/2. For a=2 => y = -1 + 7/2 = 5/2 not integer. a=4 => y = -2 + 7/2 = 3/2 not integer. So no interior point.\n\nThus with this pairing, each line covers only the column 1 point and main diagonal point, no interior points.\n\nNow try pairing (1,1)-(3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2. Equation y = (x+1)/2. For a=2 => y = 1.5 not integer. a=4 => y = 2.5 not integer. So no interior.\n\nPair (1,2)-(2,3): slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1. Equation y = x+1. For a=3 => y=4 out. So no interior.\n\nPair (1,3)-(4,1): slope = -2/3, as before, no interior.\n\nThus for n=4, any pairing yields lines that only cover the column 1 point and the main diagonal point, no interior points. So we need extra lines to cover interior points, but we only have one extra line (for D1). However, D1 is (1,4). The line through D1 and some interior point could cover at most one interior point (maybe more if collinear). But interior points are (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Could we find a line through (1,4) that passes through two of these interior points? Let's see: line through (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2, passes through (3,0) out. So only one interior point. Line through (1,4) and (2,1) slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, passes through (3,-2) out. So only one interior point. Line through (1,4) and (3,1) slope = -3/2, passes through (2,2.5) out. So only one interior point. So we can cover at most one interior point with L4. So we cannot cover all three interior points. So indeed with n=4, it's impossible to cover all points with 4 sunny lines. So the solution's claim that k=n is impossible for n>=4 seems plausible, at least for n=4. For n=5, we need to check if possible. Let's try to see if we can find a covering with 5 sunny lines for n=5.\n\nGeneral approach: For n >= 4, we have n main diagonal points, n column 1 points. Pair column 1 points (except (1,n)) with main diagonal points (except (1,n)). That yields n-1 lines each covering a column 1 point and a main diagonal point. The remaining main diagonal point (1,n) is left for the nth line. Then we have interior points: points with a>=2 and b>=2 (except those on main diagonal). Also points with a>=2, b=1 (first row except (1,n) maybe). Let's count interior points.\n\nTotal points = n(n+1)/2. Points covered by the n-1 lines (excluding the line for (1,n)) include: each of those lines covers exactly 2 points (the column 1 point and the main diagonal point). So they cover 2*(n-1) points. The line for (1,n) may also cover some other points. So total covered points so far: 2*(n-1) + maybe some extra from the nth line. But total points needed to cover: n(n+1)/2. For n=4, total points = 10. 2*(n-1) = 6. So we need to cover remaining 4 points with the nth line. But nth line can cover at most n points (one per diagonal). So maybe possible? But we need to check constraints.\n\nFor n=4, we have 6 points covered by the three lines (column 1 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3) and main diagonal points (2,3),(4,1),(3,2)). That's 6 distinct points. The remaining points are (2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(4,1?) Wait (4,1) is already covered. Actually let's list all points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). Covered by the three lines: (1,1),(2,3); (1,2),(4,1); (1,3),(3,2). So covered: (1,1),(2,3),(1,2),(4,1),(1,3),(3,2). That's 6 points. Uncovered: (1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's 4 points. The nth line (line for (1,4)) can cover at most one point per diagonal. The remaining points are on diagonals: (1,4) sum=5 (main diagonal), (2,1) sum=3, (2,2) sum=4, (3,1) sum=4? Wait (3,1) sum=4 as well. So (2,2) and (3,1) are on same diagonal sum=4. So a line cannot contain both (2,2) and (3,1) because that would be two points on same diagonal, which is allowed? Actually a sunny line cannot contain two points with same sum because that would be slope -1? Let's check: Two points (a,b) and (c,d) with a+b = c+d. The line connecting them has slope (d-b)/(c-a). Since a+b = c+d => d = a+b - c. So slope = (a+b - c - b)/(c-a) = (a - c)/(c-a) = -1. So indeed any line through two points with same sum has slope -1, which is forbidden. So a sunny line cannot contain two points from the same diagonal. So the nth line cannot contain both (2,2) and (3,1). So at most one of them can be covered by the nth line. So we have at least 3 uncovered points after using nth line: (1,4) is covered by nth line, plus at most one of (2,2) or (3,1), and maybe (2,1) if it lies on the line. But can we choose nth line to pass through (1,4) and (2,1)? That line slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, which is sunny. Does it also pass through (2,2)? No. So we can cover (1,4) and (2,1). Then we still have (2,2) and (3,1) uncovered. So at least 2 points remain uncovered. So impossible. So indeed for n=4, k=n impossible.\n\nNow for n=5, total points = 5*6/2 = 15. Column 1 points: (1,1) to (1,5). Main diagonal points: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Pair column 1 points (except (1,5)) with main diagonal points (except (1,5)). That's 4 lines covering 8 points (each line covers 2). So covered points: 8. Uncovered points: total 15 - 8 = 7 points. The remaining main diagonal point (1,5) is left for the 5th line, which can cover at most 5 points (one per diagonal). So we need to cover 7 points with that line, impossible. But maybe some of the 4 lines can cover more than 2 points each, i.e., they could also cover some interior points besides the column 1 and main diagonal point. That would reduce the number of uncovered points. So we need to see if we can design pairings such that each line passes through more interior points. That might be possible for larger n.\n\nThus the impossibility for n>=4 may be more subtle: we need to prove that with n sunny lines, you cannot cover all points. The solution's argument is insufficient (they only mention horizontal line forced). However, the final answer might still be correct (i.e., k=n impossible for n>=4). We need to verify if there exists a counterexample for n>=4 that shows k=n is possible. If we can find a construction for n=5 with all 5 sunny lines covering all points, then the answer is wrong. If not, answer may be correct.\n\nLet's attempt to construct for n=5. We need 5 distinct sunny lines covering all 15 points. Let's try to find a systematic construction.\n\nWe can think of using lines through (1,1) and each of the main diagonal points except (1,5). But those lines are vertical/horizontal? Let's check: line through (1,1) and (2,4) slope = (4-1)/(2-1) = 3, sunny. It also passes through (3,7) out. So only covers those two points. Not helpful.\n\nMaybe we can use lines that pass through multiple interior points. For example, line through (2,1) and (3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. That line also passes through (4,3) (since slope 1, intercept? Let's compute: line through (2,1) slope 1 => y = x - 1. For x=4, y=3, which is (4,3). Is (4,3) in the set? For n=5, (4,3) sum = 7 > 6, so not in set. So only (2,1),(3,2) are covered. Also (5,4) out.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny. It also passes through (3,1.5) not integer.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, sunny. It also passes through (3,?,) y = -2/3*(3-2)+3 = -2/3+3 = 7/3 not integer.\n\nMaybe we can use lines that pass through three points: e.g., line through (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) but (3,6) out.\n\nWe need to cover many points with each line. Since each line can intersect each diagonal at most once, the maximum number of points a line can cover is the number of diagonals it intersects, which is at most n (if it intersects all diagonals). But can a sunny line intersect all diagonals? Yes, a line with slope not 0, infinite, -1 will intersect each diagonal exactly once (except maybe some diagonals outside the region). Actually a line y = mx + b will intersect diagonal a+b = d at a point (x, d-x). The condition that the point lies on the line: d-x = m x + b => (m+1)x = d - b => x = (d - b)/(m+1). For each d, there is a unique x (if m != -1). So each diagonal yields a unique intersection point (maybe not integer). So a line can intersect each diagonal at most once, and may produce integer points for some diagonals. So the number of integer points on a line is limited by the number of diagonals for which (d - b)/(m+1) is integer and also yields integer y = d - x. So the number of integer points is limited by the denominator of m+1. If m is rational p/q in lowest terms, then m+1 = (p+q)/q. So x = (d - b) * q/(p+q). So integer x occurs when (d - b) is divisible by (p+q)/gcd(q,p+q). So the number of integer points is about O(n/(p+q)). So we can get many integer points if denominator small.\n\nThus we might be able to cover many points with a single line if we choose slope rational with small denominator. For instance, slope = 1 (m=1) gives m+1 = 2. Then x = (d - b)/2. So integer x occurs when d - b is even. So about half of diagonals give integer points. So a line with slope 1 can cover about n/2 points. Similarly slope = 2 (m=2) gives m+1=3, so about n/3 points.\n\nThus we might be able to cover many points with a few lines. Perhaps we can cover all points with n sunny lines for any n? Let's test for n=5. Choose lines with slope 1 passing through appropriate points to cover many points. For example, line y = x + c. For integer points (a,b) with b = a + c, and a+b <= 6 => a + (a + c) <=6 => 2a + c <=6 => a <= (6-c)/2. So for each c, we have points (a, a+c) for a from max(1,1-c) to floor((6-c)/2). So the number of points on line y = x + c is floor((6-c)/2) - max(1,1-c) + 1. For c >=0, max(1,1-c) = 1. So number = floor((6-c)/2) - 1 + 1 = floor((6-c)/2). For c=0: floor(6/2)=3 points: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). For c=1: floor(5/2)=2 points: (1,2),(2,3). For c=2: floor(4/2)=2 points: (1,3),(2,4). For c=3: floor(3/2)=1 point: (1,4). For c=4: floor(2/2)=1 point: (1,5). For c=5: floor(1/2)=0 points (none). So lines with slope 1 can cover many points.\n\nNow we need to use exactly n=5 lines, each sunny (slope not 0, infinite, -1). So slope 1 is allowed. So we could use lines y = x + c for c = 0,1,2,3,4. That's 5 lines. Let's see if they cover all points.\n\nList points in S for n=5: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4); (3,1),(3,2),(3,3); (4,1),(4,2); (5,1). That's 15 points.\n\nNow lines:\n\n- L0: y = x (c=0) covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). (4,4) out (sum 8 >6). So covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3).\n\n- L1: y = x + 1 (c=1) covers (1,2),(2,3). (3,4) out (sum 7 >6). So covers (1,2),(2,3).\n\n- L2: y = x + 2 (c=2) covers (1,3),(2,4). (3,5) out (sum 8). So covers (1,3),(2,4).\n\n- L3: y = x + 3 (c=3) covers (1,4). (2,5) out (sum 7). So covers (1,4).\n\n- L4: y = x + 4 (c=4) covers (1,5). (2,6) out. So covers (1,5).\n\nNow which points are uncovered? Let's list all points and mark covered:\n\n(1,1) covered by L0.\n(1,2) covered by L1.\n(1,3) covered by L2.\n(1,4) covered by L3.\n(1,5) covered by L4.\n(2,1) not covered yet.\n(2,2) covered by L0.\n(2,3) covered by L1.\n(2,4) covered by L2.\n(3,1) not covered.\n(3,2) not covered.\n(3,3) covered by L0.\n(4,1) not covered.\n(4,2) not covered.\n(5,1) not covered.\n\nSo we still have points: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 6 points. We have used 5 lines already; we cannot add more lines because we need exactly 5 lines. So this set of lines fails.\n\nWe could try different slopes. Perhaps we can use lines with slope 2, -2, etc. Let's try to find a set of 5 sunny lines covering all points. This is a covering problem.\n\nWe need to cover all points with exactly n lines. Since there are n(n+1)/2 points, average points per line = (n+1)/2. For n=5, average = 3. So each line must cover about 3 points on average. That's plausible.\n\nBut we need to ensure each line is sunny. Let's try to find a covering for n=5.\n\nOne approach: Use lines that each contain a column 1 point and a main diagonal point, as before, plus maybe some interior points. But we need to cover all interior points. Perhaps we can choose pairings that also include interior points.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to design a covering using lines of slope 1 and slope -2 etc.\n\nLet's try to systematically search for a covering for n=5. Since we are not supposed to re-prove, but we can try to see if a counterexample exists. However, we need to be careful not to do exhaustive search manually; but we can reason.\n\nWe need 5 lines. Let's denote lines L1,...,L5. Each line cannot be horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. So slopes allowed: any rational except 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe can think of constructing a \"grid\" of points and covering them with lines that go through many points. For n=5, the points form a triangular shape. Let's try to see if we can cover all points with 5 lines by using lines that each pass through a column 1 point and a row point, etc.\n\nWe can also think of using lines that go through the points (2,1),(3,2),(4,3),(5,4) but (5,4) out. So maybe line through (2,1) and (3,2) slope 1, which we already used (y = x - 1). That line covers (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) out, (5,4) out. So it covers two points.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, same slope, but different intercept: y = x - 2. That line covers (3,1),(4,2),(5,3) out. So covers two points.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (5,1) vertical, not allowed. So we need to cover (4,1) and (5,1) maybe with lines of slope something else.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (2,2) slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, allowed. That line also passes through (3,1.5) not integer. So covers (4,1),(2,2). (2,2) already covered by L0 maybe.\n\nLine through (5,1) and (2,3) slope = (3-1)/(2-5) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, allowed. That line also passes through (3,2)? Let's check: line through (5,1) slope -2/3: equation y - 1 = -2/3 (x - 5) => y = -2/3 x + (10/3) + 1 = -2/3 x + 13/3. For x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = 7/3 not integer. So not.\n\nWe need to cover all points. Let's try to think of a systematic covering using lines that each contain a column 1 point and a row point (i.e., a point with b=1). For each column 1 point (1,b) we could pair with a row point (a,1). The line through (1,b) and (a,1) has slope = (1-b)/(a-1). This slope is not 0 (unless b=1) and not infinite (unless a=1) and not -1 (unless (1-b) = -(a-1) => b = a). So if we pair (1,b) with (a,1) where a != b, slope is not -1. So we can pair each column 1 point (except (1,1) maybe) with a distinct row point (a,1). That would cover both points. This is similar to constructing a permutation pairing between column 1 points (except (1,1)) and row points (except (1,1)). Since there are n-1 column 1 points (b=2..n) and n-1 row points (a=2..n). We need a bijection f: {2,...,n} -> {2,...,n} such that f(b) != b (to avoid slope -1). That's a derangement of size n-1. For n>=3, derangements exist (except maybe n-1=1). For n=5, we have 4 elements, derangements exist. So we can pair (1,b) with (f(b),1). Then each line covers two points: a column point and a row point. Additionally, each line also passes through some interior points maybe.\n\nNow we also need to cover points on the main diagonal (a+b = n+1). Those are (a, n+1-a). Some of them may be already covered if they coincide with either column or row points. The main diagonal points include (1,n) (column point), (n,1) (row point). The other diagonal points (a, n+1-a) for a=2..n-1 are interior points (both coordinates >=2). They are not covered yet. So we need to cover those diagonal points too. We have n lines total. We have used n-1 lines for the pairings (column-point + row-point). That leaves one line left (the nth line). That line could be used to cover all remaining diagonal points. Is that possible? Let's see.\n\nWe have n-1 lines each covering a column point (1,b) and a row point (f(b),1). The remaining points are the diagonal points (a, n+1-a) for a=2..n-1, plus maybe some interior points not on diagonal. Let's count.\n\nTotal points: n(n+1)/2. Points covered by the n-1 lines: each line covers 2 points (column and row), but some lines may also cover interior points if the line passes through them. But at minimum, they cover 2*(n-1) points. That's 2n-2 points. The diagonal points count: n points (including (1,n) and (n,1)). Among those, (1,n) and (n,1) are already covered as column and row points. So remaining diagonal points are n-2 points. So after covering column and row points, we have uncovered at least n-2 diagonal points. Also there may be interior points not on diagonal that are not covered. Let's count interior points not on diagonal: total points - column points - row points - diagonal points (excluding (1,n) and (n,1) counted already). Column points: n points (1,b) for b=1..n. Row points: n points (a,1) for a=1..n. However, (1,1) counted twice, so total distinct column+row points = 2n - 1. Diagonal points: n points, but (1,n) and (n,1) are already in column+row sets. So distinct diagonal points not already counted = n - 2. So total distinct points = (2n - 1) + (n - 2) + interior non-diagonal points = n(n+1)/2. So interior non-diagonal points count = n(n+1)/2 - (2n - 1) - (n - 2) = n(n+1)/2 - (3n - 3) = (n^2 + n - 6n + 6)/2 = (n^2 -5n +6)/2 = (n-2)(n-3)/2. Indeed, interior non-diagonal points are those with a>=2, b>=2, a+b <= n+1, and a+b != n+1. So count = total interior points minus diagonal interior points. The interior points count (a>=2,b>=2) is sum_{a=2}^{n-1} (n+1-a - 1) = sum_{a=2}^{n-1} (n - a) = sum_{k=1}^{n-2} k = (n-2)(n-1)/2. Subtract diagonal interior points (n-2) yields (n-2)(n-1)/2 - (n-2) = (n-2)(n-1 - 2)/2 = (n-2)(n-3)/2. Yes.\n\nThus interior non-diagonal points count = (n-2)(n-3)/2. For n=5, that's (3)*(2)/2 = 3 points. Let's list them: points with a>=2,b>=2, a+b <=6, and a+b !=6. So possible points: (2,2) sum=4; (2,3) sum=5; (3,2) sum=5; (3,3) sum=6 (diagonal). So interior non-diagonal points are (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's 3 points. Indeed.\n\nNow after covering column+row points with n-1 lines, we have uncovered diagonal interior points (n-2 points) and interior non-diagonal points ((n-2)(n-3)/2 points). For n=5, uncovered points: diagonal interior points: (2,4),(3,3),(4,2) (but (3,3) is diagonal interior point? Actually (3,3) sum=6, diagonal interior point; (2,4) sum=6, diagonal interior; (4,2) sum=6). So diagonal interior points: (2,4),(3,3),(4,2) (3 points). And interior non-diagonal points: (2,2),(2,3),(3,2) (3 points). So total uncovered after n-1 lines = 6 points. We have one line left to cover these 6 points. Can a single sunny line cover all 6 points? Let's see.\n\nWe need a line that passes through all 6 points: (2,4),(3,3),(4,2) (diagonal) and (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's impossible because a line cannot contain two points from the same diagonal (except if slope -1). But (2,4) and (4,2) are on diagonal sum=6, so they cannot be on same sunny line. So we cannot cover all diagonal interior points with a single sunny line. So we need more than one line to cover diagonal interior points. But we only have one line left. So the pairing approach fails.\n\nThus we need a different approach that uses lines that cover more than two points, perhaps covering multiple diagonal points with different lines. But we have only n lines total. So we need to allocate lines to cover diagonal points and interior points efficiently.\n\nGiven the constraints, maybe it's impossible for n>=4 to have all n lines sunny. The solution's argument is insufficient but the conclusion may be correct. However, we need to verify if there is any known solution for this problem. I recall a known problem: \"Find all possible numbers of sunny lines\" might have answer: all k from 0 to n-2 inclusive, plus k=n-1 always possible, and k=n only possible for n=3. This seems plausible. I think I've seen a similar problem. So the answer might be correct.\n\nWe need to verify if the solution's reasoning is valid. The solution provides constructions for k in [0,n-2] (construction using two non-sunny lines x=1 and y=1, plus vertical lines x=2,...,x=n-k-1, and then sunny lines through (1,1) and each uncovered point). Let's examine that construction.\n\nConstruction for 0 <= k <= n-2:\n\n- Non-sunny backbone: x=1 and y=1 (vertical and horizontal lines). These are non-sunny (vertical/horizontal). They cover column 1 and row 1.\n\n- Additional non-sunny vertical lines: for a = 2,3,..., n-k-1, add line x = a. So total non-sunny lines: 2 + (n-k-2) = n-k. Indeed, we need n-k non-sunny lines. So we have vertical lines x=2,...,x=n-k-1 (if n-k-1 >= 2). For k <= n-2, n-k >= 2, so there is at least one vertical line? Let's check: If k = n-2, then n-k = 2, so we have only the two non-sunny lines x=1 and y=1, and no additional vertical lines (since a runs from 2 to n-k-1 = n-(n-2)-1 = 1? Actually n-k-1 = n - (n-2) - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1, so range a=2..1 empty). So exactly 2 non-sunny lines, which is okay.\n\n- After adding those vertical lines, they claim that the only uncovered points are Q_{n,k} = { (a,b) | a >= n-k, b >= 2, a+b <= n+1 }. Let's verify.\n\nThe non-sunny lines cover:\n\n- x=1: all points with a=1, any b (b from 1 to n). So column 1 covered.\n\n- y=1: all points with b=1, any a (a from 1 to n). So row 1 covered.\n\n- x = a for a = 2,..., n-k-1: these vertical lines cover all points with a in that range, any b (subject to a+b <= n+1). So they cover columns 2 through n-k-1 fully.\n\nThus the uncovered columns are a = n-k, n-k+1, ..., n. But also note that column n is only point (n,1) which is covered by y=1 (since b=1). So column n is covered. Actually column n has only point (n,1) which is covered by y=1. So column n is covered. So uncovered columns are a = n-k, n-k+1, ..., n-1? Let's check.\n\nThe vertical lines cover columns up to n-k-1. So columns a >= n-k are not covered by vertical lines. However, column a = n-k may have points with b >= 2 (since b=1 is covered by y=1). So uncovered points are those with a >= n-k, b >= 2, and a+b <= n+1. This matches Q_{n,k}.\n\nNow they claim that |Q_{n,k}| = k. Let's compute size of Q_{n,k}. For a >= n-k, a <= n (since a <= n). For each a, b runs from max(2, 1) to n+1-a. Since a >= n-k, n+1-a <= n+1-(n-k) = k+1. So b <= k+1. Also b >= 2. So for each a, b runs from 2 to k+1-a'? Wait careful: For a fixed a, the condition a+b <= n+1 => b <= n+1-a. Since a >= n-k, n+1-a <= k+1. So b <= min(k+1, n+1-a). But also b >= 2. So number of b values for each a is max(0, min(k+1, n+1-a) - 1). Let's compute for a = n-k: n+1-a = n+1-(n-k) = k+1. So b <= k+1, b >= 2 => b values = (k+1) - 2 + 1 = k? Actually count = (k+1) - 2 + 1 = k? Let's compute: from 2 to k+1 inclusive => number = (k+1) - 2 + 1 = k. So for a = n-k, we have k points: (n-k,2),(n-k,3),...,(n-k,k+1). For a = n-k+1: n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+1) = k. So b <= k, b >=2 => b values from 2 to k inclusive => count = k-2+1 = k-1. For a = n-k+2: n+1-a = k-1 => b from 2 to k-1 => count = (k-1)-2+1 = k-2. Continue until a = n-1: n+1-a = 2 => b from 2 to 2 => count = 1. For a = n: n+1-a = 1 => b <=1, but b >=2 => no solutions. So total points = k + (k-1) + (k-2) + ... + 1 = k(k+1)/2? Actually sum from i=1 to k of i = k(k+1)/2. Wait we have counts: for a = n-k, count = k; for a = n-k+1, count = k-1; ... for a = n-1, count = 1. So sum = k + (k-1) + ... + 1 = k(k+1)/2. That's not equal to k unless k=1 or k=0. So the claim that |Q_{n,k}| = k is incorrect. Let's double-check: maybe they defined Q_{n,k} differently: \"the only uncovered points are Q_{n,k} = { (a,b) | a >= n-k, b >= 2, a+b <= n+1 }\". Let's compute for n=5, k=2. Then n-k = 3. So Q_{5,2} = { (a,b) | a >= 3, b >= 2, a+b <= 6 }. Let's list: a=3 => b <= 3 => b=2,3 => points (3,2),(3,3). a=4 => b <= 2 => b=2 => (4,2). a=5 => b <= 1 => no b >=2. So Q = {(3,2),(3,3),(4,2)} => 3 points. That's not equal to k=2. So indeed |Q| = 3 > k. So the claim that |Q| = k is false.\n\nBut maybe they meant something else: They said \"One checks that |Q_{n,k}| = k; indeed the column a=n-k contributes the points (n-k,2), (n-k,3), ..., (n-k,k+1) and every other column already contains a point of the backbone.\" Wait they claim that every other column already contains a point of the backbone. Let's examine: The backbone includes x=1, y=1, and vertical lines x=2,...,x=n-k-1. So columns a = 2,...,n-k-1 are covered fully. Column a = n-k is not covered by vertical lines (since vertical lines go up to n-k-1). So column a = n-k is partially uncovered: points with b >=2 are uncovered. Column a = n-k+1,..., n-1 are also not covered by vertical lines. However, they claim that \"every other column already contains a point of the backbone\". But column a = n-k+1,...,n-1 have points (a,1) covered by y=1 (since b=1). So they have at least one point covered (the point (a,1)). But they may have other points (a,b) with b >= 2 that are uncovered. However, maybe they claim that those points are covered by the line through (1,1) and (a,b) (the sunny line). But they haven't added those lines yet. The Q_{n,k} set includes points with a >= n-k, b >= 2, a+b <= n+1. So for a > n-k, there are points with b >= 2 that satisfy a+b <= n+1. For a = n-k+1, b <= n+1-(n-k+1) = k. So b can be 2,...,k. So there are k-1 points in column a = n-k+1. So Q_{n,k} includes those points. So Q_{n,k} size is indeed sum_{i=1}^{k} i = k(k+1)/2. So the claim that |Q_{n,k}|=k is false. So the construction fails to produce exactly k sunny lines? Wait they claim they will draw a sunny line for each point in Q_{n,k}, giving exactly k sunny lines. But Q_{n,k} has more than k points (for k>1). So they would draw more than k lines, exceeding n lines. However, they claim they will draw the line through each (a,b) in Q_{n,k} and the fixed point (1,1). That would be one line per point, but many of those lines may coincide? Let's examine: For each (a,b) in Q_{n,k}, they draw line through (a,b) and (1,1). Since (1,1) is fixed, each line is determined by the point (a,b). If two points (a,b) and (a',b') produce the same line through (1,1), then they must be collinear with (1,1), i.e., (a,b) and (a',b') lie on same line through (1,1). That would happen if the slopes 