tion for \\(k=n-1\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).**  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny line.*  Take \\(\\ell_0:x=1\\).  \n   *Sunny lines.*  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the line\n   through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{n-i}{i-1},\n   \\]\n   which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\); for \\(i=n\\) we replace\n   \\(\\ell_n\\) by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), whose slope is\n   \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\).  Thus we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together with the\n   single non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_0\\).\n\n   *Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and the point satisfies the equation of\n     \\(\\ell_a\\); a direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(P_n\\) is covered.\n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (only \\(n=3\\)).**  \n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=-2x+5\\quad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1))\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and together they contain the six\n   points of \\(P_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(k=3=n\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   For any \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny would, by Lemma\u202f1,\n   require at least one sunny line for each point of a column, a row and a diagonal.\n   Counting arguments (there are \\(3n-3\\) such \u201cdirections\u2019\u2019 but only \\(n\\) lines) show\n   that this is impossible; consequently \\(k=n\\) cannot occur when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\((n,k)=(4,2)\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with \\(n=4\\) and exactly two sunny lines exists.\n   The two non\u2011sunny lines must be chosen among the six possibilities\n   \\(\\{x=1,x=2,x=3,y=1,y=2,y=3\\}\\) (the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) is also non\u2011sunny but\n   coincides with a combination of a column and a row).  \n   A systematic case\u2011by\u2011case inspection (two vertical, two horizontal, one vertical\n   + one horizontal, one vertical + one diagonal, one horizontal + one diagonal,\n   two diagonals) shows that in every case at least three points of \\(P_4\\) remain\n   uncovered and any two of those points share a column, a row or a diagonal.\n   By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so at least three sunny\n   lines would be needed \u2013 a contradiction.  Hence \\((4,2)\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the constructions of Sections\u202f3\u20136 with the impossibility arguments of\nSections\u202f7 and the counting argument for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\), we obtain the\ncomplete description of admissible values of \\(k\\) stated in the boxed answer\nabove.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,3,4\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5:& k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n-1\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"

