\[\n   m_i=\\frac{n-i}{i-1},\n   \\]\n   which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\); for \\(i=n\\) we replace\n   \\(\\ell_n\\) by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\)).\n   Thus we have exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together with the single non\u2011sunny line\n   \\(\\ell_0\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and a direct substitution shows that\n     \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\); hence it lies on the sunny line\n     \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n   Every point of \\(P_n\\) is therefore covered and exactly \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (only \\(n=3\\)).**  \n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=-2x+5\\quad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1))\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and cover the six points of \\(P_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(k=3=n\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   For any \\(n\\ge4\\) a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(P_n\\)\n   (Lemma\u202f1 together with the fact that in \\(P_n\\) any three points have two with\n   equal \\(x\\), equal \\(y\\) or equal \\(x+y\\)).  With \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at\n   most \\(2n\\) points, but \\(|P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).  Thus \\(k=n\\) is\n   impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\((n,k)=(4,4)\\).**  \n\n   For \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|P_4|=10\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain at most two\n   points of \\(P_4\\); a short case analysis shows that no sunny line can contain three\n   points (any three points of \\(P_4\\) necessarily share a column, a row or a diagonal).\n   Consequently four sunny lines can cover at most \\(8\\) points, leaving at least two\n   points uncovered.  Since no non\u2011sunny line is allowed (all four lines are sunny),\n   the covering condition cannot be satisfied.  Hence \\(k=4\\) is impossible for\n   \\(n=4\\).\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\((n,k)=(4,2)\\).**  \n   The argument of Step\u202f4 already shows that the construction fails for \\((4,2)\\).\n   A direct exhaustive check of all possibilities for the two non\u2011sunny lines\n   (vertical, horizontal or the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\)) reveals that after their removal\n   at least three points of \\(P_4\\) remain uncovered, and by Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can\n   cover at most one of them.  Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed,\n   contradicting \\(k=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the explicit constructions of Sections\u202f3\u20136 with the impossibility\narguments of Sections\u202f7\u20138 yields the complete description of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) stated in the boxed answer above.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5:& k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n-1\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"

