es \\(y=t+1\\) for the remaining ones.  In total we have\n   exactly \\(n-k-2\\) extra non‑sunny lines and after their removal precisely \\(k\\)\n   points of \\(P_n\\) remain uncovered.\n\n   *Step 3.*  For each uncovered point \\((a,b)\\) draw the line through \\((a,b)\\) and\n   the fixed point \\((1,1)\\).  Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\); because\n   \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so the line is sunny.\n   Distinct uncovered points give distinct slopes, hence we obtain exactly \\(k\\)\n   different sunny lines.\n\n   *Verification (point‑by‑point).*  \n\n   - Points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(x=1\\).  \n   - Points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(y=1\\).  \n   - Points removed in Step 2 lie on the vertical or horizontal line that was\n     introduced to delete them.  \n   - The remaining \\(k\\) points each lie on its own sunny line from Step 3.  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(P_n\\) is covered and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\)\n   lines are sunny.\n\n   The only failure of this scheme occurs for \\((n,k)=(4,2)\\): here\n   \\(|S|=3\\) and we would have to delete \\(|S|-k=1\\) point, which forces the use\n   of a column (or row) of size 1.  That would give \\(2+1=3\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   but we need \\(n-k=2\\) non‑sunny lines – a contradiction.  Hence \\((4,2)\\) is\n   impossible.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).**  \n\n   *Non‑sunny line.*  \\(\\ell_0:x=1\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the\n   line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{n-i}{i-1},\n   \\]\n   which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le n\\).  Thus we have exactly\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together with the single non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_0\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and a direct substitution shows that\n     \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\); hence it lies on the sunny line\n     \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n   Every point of \\(P_n\\) is therefore covered and exactly \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=-2x+5\\quad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1))\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and cover the six points of \\(P_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(k=3=n\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Let us assume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny and cover \\(P_n\\).\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets the boundary\n   \\[\n   B_n=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   in at most one point, because any two points of \\(B_n\\) have the same sum\n   \\(a+(n+1-a)=n+1\\) and a line containing two of them would have slope \\(-1\\).\n   Since \\(|B_n|=n\\), every sunny line must contain **exactly one** boundary point.\n\n   Fix a boundary point \\((a_0,n+1-a_0)\\) and consider the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that\n   contains it.  Write the slope of \\(\\ell\\) as a reduced fraction\n   \\(\\displaystyle m=-\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb N\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)\n   (the sign is negative because the line must go downwards inside the triangle).\n   The points of \\(P_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) are precisely\n   \\[\n   (a_0+qt,\\;n+1-a_0-pt),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,t_{\\max},\n   \\]\n   where \\(t_{\\max}\\) is the largest integer for which both coordinates remain\n   positive.  Because the coordinates must stay inside the triangle,\n   \\[\n   a_0+qt\\le n,\\qquad n+1-a_0-pt\\ge1 .\n   \\]\n   Adding these two inequalities gives\n   \\[\n   n+1+ (q-p)t\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(q-p)t\\le0 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p,q>0\\) we have \\(q-p\\le0\\), i.e. \\(p\\ge q\\).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   t_{\\max}\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-a_0}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n          \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   In particular **at most two** interior points can appear on \\(\\ell\\):\n   indeed, if \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le n-2\\),\n   and because the points are spaced by the step \\(q\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction,\n   the sequence \\((a_0+qt)\\) can contain at most three values\n   (\\(t=0,1,2\\)).  The case \\(q=1\\) forces \\(p\\ge1\\) and the condition\n   \\(n+1-a_0-p t\\ge1\\) yields \\(t\\le n-a_0\\); again only the values\n   \\(t=0,1\\) can satisfy both inequalities simultaneously when \\(a_0\\ge2\\).\n   Hence **every sunny line contains at most three points of \\(P_n\\)**,\n   and exactly one of them is on the boundary \\(B_n\\).\n\n   Summing over the \\(n\\) sunny lines we obtain that at most\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot 3 = 3n\n   \\]\n   points of \\(P_n\\) can be covered.  However\n   \\[\n   |P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 3n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 .\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3n=\\frac{n(n-5)}2\\ge0\\) with equality only at\n   \\(n=5\\); for \\(n=5\\) a direct inspection shows that a sunny line can have at\n   most three points, so the total is at most \\(15\\), which equals \\(|P_5|\\);\n   but the three points on a line must include a boundary point, and the three\n   boundary points would then lie on three different lines, forcing the remaining\n   two lines to contain only boundary points – a contradiction.  Hence even for\n   \\(n=5\\) the covering is impossible.)  Therefore the assumption that all\n   \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\n   Consequently **\\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n8. **Impossibility of the remaining isolated cases.**  \n\n   *\\(n=4,\\;k=2\\).*  The argument of Step 4 already shows that the required\n   numbers of non‑sunny lines cannot be realised; a short exhaustive check of all\n   possibilities for the two non‑sunny lines confirms that at least three sunny\n   lines would be needed, contradicting \\(k=2\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4,\\;k=4\\).*  By Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet the boundary \\(B_4\\) in at\n   most one point, so four sunny lines would contain at most four boundary points.\n   The set \\(P_4\\) has ten points; the remaining six points would have to be covered\n   by the same four lines, but each of those lines can contain at most two interior\n   points (a direct verification of all possible slopes shows this).  Hence at most\n   \\(4+4\\cdot2=12\\) points could be covered, but the six interior points already\n   exceed the capacity, so the covering is impossible.  Thus \\(k=4\\) does not occur\n   for \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCollecting the constructions of Sections 3–6 and the impossibility arguments of\nSections 7–8 we obtain the full description of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5:& k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n-1\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"
