teger points, we need a rational slope and intercept such that for each anti-diagonal D_s, the intersection point has integer coordinates. This is a challenging Diophantine condition.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to find a configuration for n=6 manually using computer search. But we cannot run code. However, we can attempt to reason.\n\nWe need to cover D_6 (points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)). Each of the 5 sunny lines must contain exactly one of these points. So we can assign each line to one of these points. Let's denote L_i passes through P_i = (i,6-i) for i=1,...,5. Then we need to define slopes m_i such that each line also passes through integer points on other anti-diagonals.\n\nWe can try to choose slopes m_i such that each line passes through a point on D_5 (points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)). Since there are 4 points and 5 lines, we can assign each D_5 point to a distinct line, leaving one line not covering D_5 (i.e., its intersection with D_5 is a point that is already covered by another line). But we need to ensure that for each line, the intersection point on D_5 is integer.\n\nLet's try to assign slopes manually. For line L_i passing through P_i = (i,6-i). Suppose we want L_i to also pass through Q_j = (j,5-j) for some j. Then the line through (i,6-i) and (j,5-j) has slope m = ((6-i)-(5-j))/(i-j) = (1 - i + j)/(i-j) = (j - i + 1)/(i - j) = -(j - i + 1)/(j - i) = -1 - 1/(j - i). That's the same formula as earlier. So the slope is m = -1 - 1/(j - i). This slope is sunny as long as j - i ≠ 0 (vertical) and j - i ≠ -1 (horizontal). So we need j - i ≠ 0, -1. So j cannot be i or i-1.\n\nThus for each L_i, we can choose any j ∈ {1,...,4} \\ {i,i-1} to define a line that passes through P_i and Q_j. That line will be sunny. So we can assign each L_i to a distinct Q_j, as long as we avoid the forbidden pairs. This is exactly the bipartite graph we described earlier. So we need a matching from left vertices i=1,...,5 to right vertices j=1,...,4 such that j ≠ i and