s. Could they be covered by a sunny line through (1,1) that also passes through (1,2) or (2,1)? Let's see: line through (1,1) and (1,2) is vertical (x=1), which is non-sunny. Through (1,1) and (2,1) is horizontal (y=1), also non-sunny. So those are non-sunny. So we cannot replace ℓ_3 with a sunny line through (1,1) that also covers (1,2) or (2,1). Could we use a sunny line through (2,2) that also passes through (1,2) or (2,1)? Let's check: line through (2,2) and (1,2) is horizontal (y=2), non-sunny. Through (2,2) and (2,1) is vertical (x=2), non-sunny. So not sunny.\n\nThus ℓ_3 cannot be replaced by a sunny line without losing coverage of (1,2) and (2,1). So ℓ_3 must stay non-sunny. So k cannot be n (i.e., cannot have all lines sunny) because ℓ_3 cannot be replaced. So for n=4, k cannot be 4. So the solution's claim that any k from 0 to n is possible for n≥4 is false.\n\nBut maybe there is a different construction that does not start from diagonals but uses other lines to cover all points, allowing all lines sunny. Let's explore if we can have all lines sunny for n=4.\n\nGoal: 4 sunny lines covering all 10 points. Let's try to find a covering.\n\nWe need to cover points (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,1), (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1). Let's try to find 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe can consider using lines of slope 1: y = x + c. For c = 0, line y=x covers (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) but (3,3) not in region (sum=6 >5). So only (1,1), (2,2). That's two points.\n\nc = 1: y = x + 1 covers (1,2), (2,3), (3,4) (3,4 sum=7 >5) so only (1,2), (2,3). That's two points.\n\nc = -1: y = x - 1 covers (2,1), (3,2), (4,3) (4,3 sum=7 >5) so only (2,1), (3,2). Two points.\n\nc = 2: y = x + 2 covers (1,3), (2,4) (2,4 sum=6 >5) so only (1,3). One point.\n\nc = -2: y = x - 2 covers (3,1), (4,2) (4,2 sum=6 >5) so only (3,1). One point.\n\nc = 3: y = x + 3 covers (1,4) only.\n\nc = -3: y = x - 3 covers (4,1) only.\n\nThus using slope 1 lines, we need at least 7 lines to cover all points (since each line covers at most 2 points except some cover 1). So not possible with 4 lines.\n\nMaybe we can use lines of slope 2 or other slopes to cover more points per line.\n\nLet's consider line through (1,1) and (2,3): slope (3-1)/(2-1) = 2. This line equation: y-1 = 2(x-1) => y = 2x -1. Check which points it passes through: For x=1, y=1; x=2, y=3; x=3, y=5 (not in region). So covers (1,1) and (2,3). That's good.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 0.5 = 1/2. Equation: y-1 = (1/2)(x-1) => y = (x+1)/2. For x=1, y=1; x=3, y=2; x=5, y=3 (not in region). So covers (1,1) and (3,2). That's good.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (4,1): slope 0 (horizontal), not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (1,4): vertical, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (2,2): slope 1 (sunny). Already considered.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (3,1): slope 0 (horizontal), not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (1,2): vertical.\n\nThus we can cover (1,1) with many sunny lines, but we need to cover all points. Let's try to design 4 sunny lines that cover all points.\n\nWe have 10 points. Each line can cover at most maybe 3 points? Let's see.\n\nConsider line y = 2x - 1 (through (1,1) and (2,3)). It covers (1,1) and (2,3). Does it also cover (3,5) (outside). So only 2 points.\n\nLine y = (x+1)/2 (through (1,1) and (3,2)). It covers (1,1) and (3,2). Also maybe (5,3) (outside). So 2 points.\n\nLine y = -x + 5 (slope -1) not sunny. So we cannot use that.\n\nLine y = -2x + something? Let's try line through (1,4) and (2,2): slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2. Equation: y - 4 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x + 6. Check points: (1,4) works, (2,2) works, (3,0) not in region. So covers (1,4) and (2,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine through (1,3) and (3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,1): horizontal, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-2)=1, sunny. Equation: y-1 = (x-2) => y = x -1. Points: (2,1), (3,2), (4,3) (outside). So covers (2,1) and (3,2). But (3,2) already covered by earlier line maybe.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (1,2): slope (2-1)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (1,3): slope (3-1)/(1-2) = 2/(-1) = -2, sunny. Equation: y-1 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x +5. Points: (2,1), (1,3), (3,-1) (outside). So covers (2,1) and (1,3). Good.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,1): horizontal, not sunny.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (4,1): horizontal.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (1,4): slope (4-1)/(1-3) = 3/(-2) = -1.5, not -1, not 0, not infinite, so sunny. Equation: y-1 = -1.5(x-3) => y = -1.5x + 5.5. Check integer points: (3,1) works, (1,4) works? Plug x=1: y = -1.5 +5.5 = 4, yes. So line passes through (3,1) and (1,4). It also passes through (2,? ) x=2 => y = -3 +5.5 = 2.5 (non-integer). So covers (3,1) and (1,4). Good.\n\nThus we have many possible lines covering pairs of points.\n\nGoal: Choose 4 lines covering all 10 points. Let's try to find a covering manually.\n\nWe can consider using lines that each cover 2 points, then 4 lines would cover at most 8 points, insufficient. So at least one line must cover at least 3 points. Is there a line that covers 3 points in this set? Let's search.\n\nWe need a line that passes through three of the points. Since points are integer coordinates, we can search for collinear triples.\n\nCheck triples:\n\n- (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) but (3,3) not in region. So only two.\n\n- (1,2), (2,3), (3,4) but (3,4) sum=7 >5, not in region. So only two.\n\n- (2,1), (3,2), (4,3) but (4,3) sum=7 >5, not in region. So only two.\n\n- (1,3), (2,2), (3,1) are collinear? Let's check slope between (1,3) and (2,2) = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, slope -1, which is not sunny. So line slope -1, not allowed for sunny lines but allowed for non-sunny lines. But we want sunny lines maybe.\n\n- (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1) are collinear with slope -1. That's non-sunny.\n\n- (1,4), (2,2) slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. Check if (3,0) is in set? No. So only two.\n\n- (1,4), (3,1) slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. Does it also pass through (2,? ) x=2 => y = -1.5*2 + something? Let's compute line equation: using points (1,4) and (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5. Equation: y-4 = -1.5(x-1) => y = -1.5x + 5.5. At x=2, y = -3 +5.5 = 2.5, not integer. So only two points.\n\n- (1,3), (2,1) slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. Does it also pass through (3,-1) not in set.\n\n- (2,3), (3,1) slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. Does it also pass through (1,5) not in set.\n\n- (1,2), (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Check if (2,1.5) not integer.\n\nThus no line covers 3 points among these 10 points except possibly the diagonal lines of slope -1 which are non-sunny. So any sunny line can cover at most 2 points from this set. Let's verify if any sunny line could cover 3 points with integer coordinates? Possibly a line with slope 2/3 or 3/2 could pass through three integer points within the region. Let's search systematically.\n\nWe need three points (a,b), (c,d), (e,f) with a+b ≤5, c+d ≤5, e+f ≤5, all distinct, collinear, slope not 0, not infinite, not -1.\n\nWe can try to find collinear triples with small slopes.\n\nGeneral condition: (b - a)/(a - a?) Wait slope m = (d - b)/(c - a) = (f - d)/(e - c). For integer points, slope rational.\n\nLet's enumerate all points again:\n\n(1,1)\n(1,2)\n(2,1)\n(1,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,1)\n(1,4)\n(2,3)\n(3,2)\n(4,1)\n\nNow check collinear triples:\n\nWe can consider lines through (1,1) and (2,3) slope 2. Already considered. Does it also pass through (3,5) not in set. So only two.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5. Does it also pass through (5,3) not in set. So only two.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (4,1) slope 0, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (1,2) vertical, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (2,2) slope 1, sunny. Does it also pass through (3,3) not in set.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (3,1) slope 0, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (2,1) slope 0, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (1,3) vertical.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (2,2) slope 1, only two.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5, only two.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (2,3) slope 2, only two.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5, we already.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (4,1) slope 0.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,3) slope 1, sunny. Does it also pass through (3,4) not in set.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5. Does it also pass through (5,0) not in set.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,1) slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (4,1) slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, not -1, not 0, not infinite, sunny. Check if (2,? ) x=2 => y =? Let's compute line equation: points (1,2) and (4,1). Slope = -1/3. Equation: y-2 = -1/3 (x-1) => y = -x/3 + 2 + 1/3 = -x/3 + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer. At x=3, y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3 ≈1.333, not integer. So only two points.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (3,2) slope 0, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,2) slope 0, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,2) horizontal.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,4) not in set.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (3,2) slope 1, sunny. Already considered.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,1) horizontal.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (1,3) slope -2, sunny.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (1,4) slope -3, sunny.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (3,1) horizontal.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,1) horizontal.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (4,1) slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Check if (3,1.5) not integer.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (1,3) slope (3-2)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (3,2) slope 0, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (1,4) slope (4-2)/(1-2) = 2/(-1) = -2, sunny.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (3,2) slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (1,4) slope (4-3)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (4,1) horizontal.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (1,4) slope -1.5,